A1. System used for surreptitious communication. ~ CODE
A4. This is just a little poke in the ribs. ~ NUDGE
A8. This should be taken when your mother-in-law arrives. ~ TRIP
A12. Married men hold their wives in this. ~ AWE
A14. A correlative to neither or not. ~ NOR
A16. This is safe only when down and out. ~ ACE
A17. This often calls for an electric treatment. ~ VERDICT
A18. These would go further if it weren't for the tax. ~ INCOMES
A19. Where money comes from—originally. (Ask any gold-digger.) ~ ORE
A21. This girl slipped on an apple. ~ EVE
A22. No dead one could be this. ~ ALIVE
A23. An inhabitant of Fairy Land. ~ ELF
A26. Just a little deer. ~ DOE
A29. To buy or purchase back. ~ REDEEM
A33. What every girl wants to be. (If she'd just put two and two together she'd get this.) ~ KISSED
A36. Conjunction. ~ OR
A37. To bite off by degrees. ~ GNAW
A38. Where fodder goes for the winter. ~ SILO
A39. What Dempsey took at Philadelphia. ~ KO
A40. Where barkeepers go on their days off. ~ SALOON
A43. A busybody. ~ DANCER
A45. This is tanked on motor trips. ~ GAS
A47. This is a great cut-up when it has an edge on ~ AXE
A48. This makes the Babe laugh. ~ HOMER
A49. This is often seen with Flo. ~ EBB
A52. A good place for people on the water wagon. ~ SPA
A55. One who agrees—does this. ~ ACCEDES
A57. A good thing to be in a World War. ~ NEUTRAL
A59. A beastly place. ~ ZOO
A60. International Whisky Congress (init.). ~ IWC
A62. A judge of Israel (Biblical). ~ ELI
A63. A marked territory. ~ ZONE
A64. This is always fast. ~ SPEED
A65. A rose supporter. ~ STEM

D1. Subterranean depression. (Careful now, this is deep stuff.) ~ CAVE
D2. This means you are bound to pay. ~ OWE
D3. Received from a source or origin. ~ DERIVED
D5. Not disclosed. ~ UNTOLD
D6. Accomplish. ~ DO
D7. You must be sad to do this. ~ GRIEVE
D9. What the wandering boy does. ~ RAMBLES
D10. Hard drink—if you like the stuff. (This is very tricky, so think hard.) ~ ICE
D11. Any kid but your own. ~ PEST
D13. You've got to hand it to the waiter. ~ TIP
D15. What actors should learn to do. ~ ACT
D20. An Irishman's picnic. ~ RIOT
D24. It takes a very, very brave pedestrian to do this. ~ CROSS
D25. This—and three men make a quartet. ~ TENOR
D27. A shrub with bluish flowers. ~ LILAC
D28. You'd just love to do this. ~ ADORE
D30. A long period. ~ ERA
D31. Self-consciousness. ~ EGO
D32. A wife's husband. ~ MAN
D33. A sheepish little one. ~ KID
D34. So's your father to his old man. (It will take a bright son to get this one.) ~ SON
D35. To supply what is wanted. ~ EKE
D41. This is very wordy. (You'll be able to find this in the dictionary.) ~ LEXICON
D42. A home wrecker. ~ VAMP
D44. Rendezvous for wine, women and voice culture. ~ CABARET
D45. A back fence blah-blah artist. ~ GOSSIP
D46. A spirited affair. ~ SEANCE
D50. The big noise on Broadway. ~ JAZZ
D51. To put two and two together. ~ ADD
D53. A tree fruit—or human cuckoo. ~ NUT
D54. It's hard to get like this if you live off the fat of the land. ~ SLIM
D56. A love call. ~ COO
D58. This is put down in Canada. (It would be here too, if we could find any.) ~ ALE
D61. You and me. ~ WE


